wd_title:"White Days (Ayyām al-Bīḍ)",
wd_intro:"The White Days are the 13th, 14th and 15th of each Hijri month, named after the bright nights around the full moon. Fasting them is a voluntary sunnah, not an obligation. They are marked in gold on the calendar.",
wd_next:"Next White Days",wd_ev:"Evidence (English translations as given on sunnah.com)",
wd_h1:"Qatadah ibn Milhan: “The Messenger of Allah (ﷺ) used to command us to fast the days of the white (nights): thirteenth, fourteenth and fifteenth of the month. He said: This is like keeping perpetual fast.”",
wd_r1:"Sunan Abi Dawud 2449 · graded sahih by al-Albani",
wd_h2:"Abu Hurayrah: “My friend (the Prophet (ﷺ)) advised me to observe three things: (1) to fast three days a month; (2) to pray two rak'at of Duha prayer; and (3) to pray witr before sleeping.” Al-Bukhari placed it under the chapter “To fast the 13th, 14th and 15th of the lunar months”; the hadith itself says three days a month.",
wd_r2:"Sahih al-Bukhari 1981",
wd_h3:"Mu'adhah al-'Adawiyyah asked 'A'ishah whether the Prophet (ﷺ) fasted three days every month. She said yes, and that “he was not particular about the days of the month on which to observe fast.” So any three days of a month also follow the sunnah; the White Days are a recommended choice.",
wd_r3:"Sahih Muslim 1160",
wd_h4:"Nubayshah al-Hudhali: “The days of Tashriq are the days of eating and drinking.” Fasting on them is not allowed, which is why 13 Dhul-Hijjah is not fasted.",
